      faqs: [
        {
          question: 'How does ClickMasters handle DSGVO compliance for German clients?',
          answer: 'DSGVO compliance for German clients: ClickMasters signs an Auftragsverarbeitungsvertrag (AVV  Data Processing Agreement per DSGVO Article 28) before any engagement involving personal data of German or EU residents. The AVV covers: the subject matter, duration, nature, and purpose of processing; the types of personal data and categories of data subjects; the obligations and rights of the controller; technical and organisational measures; conditions for sub-processing; assistance with data subject rights requests; deletion or return of data at engagement end; and audit rights. ClickMasters maintains sub-processor list (current IT infrastructure sub-processors with their data locations), implements technical and organisational measures (TOMs) that satisfy the DSGVO Article 32 security requirements (encryption, pseudonymisation, access control, integrity, availability), and participates in data subject rights fulfilment within the required timeframes. German clients receive the AVV for review by their Datenschutzbeauftragter (Data Protection Officer) before the engagement contract is signed.',
        },
        {
          question: 'What is BSI IT-Grundschutz and does ClickMasters implement it?',
          answer: 'BSI IT-Grundschutz (IT Baseline Protection) is the German Federal Office for Information Security\'s comprehensive information security framework  the German equivalent of ISO 27001, published by the BSI as the IT-Grundschutz Kompendium. It is mandatory for German federal government IT systems and widely adopted by German regulated industry (financial services, healthcare, energy, transport). IT-Grundschutz defines: building blocks (Bausteine) covering all areas of IT security (applications, systems, networks, infrastructure, personnel), standard safeguards (Standard-Absicherung) and core safeguards (Kern-Absicherung) for organisations at different maturity levels, and the IT-Grundschutz certification process (equivalent to ISO 27001 certification). ClickMasters aligns enterprise security engagements with the relevant IT-Grundschutz building blocks: CON.1 (cryptography), OPS.1.1.3 (patch management), OPS.1.2.6 (privacy protection), APP.1.1 (Office products), APP.3.1 (web applications), INF.1 (general buildings), NET.1.2 (network management). For German clients requiring IT-Grundschutz certification, ClickMasters provides the technical documentation required for the certification process.',
        },
        {
          question: 'What is the EU AI Act and how does it affect German AI projects?',
          answer: 'The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) is the world\'s first binding AI regulation, applying to all AI systems placed on the EU market  including German companies. Key provisions: prohibited AI practices (effective February 2025  social scoring by public authorities, real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces, AI that exploits vulnerabilities of persons), high-risk AI systems (Annex III categories  biometric identification, critical infrastructure, education and vocational training, employment, essential services, law enforcement, migration, justice  require conformity assessment, technical documentation, CE marking, human oversight, and registration in the EU database), limited-risk AI systems (chatbots, deepfakes  transparency obligations: disclose that the user is interacting with AI), and minimal-risk AI systems (spam filters, AI in video games  no specific requirements). German companies deploying high-risk AI systems must: conduct a Conformitaetsbewertung (conformity assessment), maintain technical documentation (Technische Dokumentation), implement a Qualitaetsmanagementsystem (QMS), and register in the EU AI database. ClickMasters helps German clients identify whether their AI systems fall into high-risk categories and designs the technical documentation and oversight mechanisms required for compliance.',
        },
        {
          question: 'What are Germany\'s specific requirements for website cookie consent?',
          answer: 'Germany has the strictest cookie consent requirements in the EU. The TTDSG (Telekommunikation-Telemedien-Datenschutz-Gesetz, in force since December 2021) implements the ePrivacy Directive and requires opt-in consent for all cookies and tracking technologies that are not strictly necessary for the service. German supervisory authorities have specifically ruled on several practices: Google Analytics requires consent (the German DSK ruled in 2022 that Google Analytics 4 transfers personal data to the US and requires explicit consent), consent walls are prohibited (making access to content conditional on cookie consent is prohibited by the German supervisory authorities  a cookie banner that says \'accept or leave the site\' violates German law), pre-ticked boxes are prohibited (consent must be freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous  pre-ticked \'I agree\' boxes do not meet this standard), and reject must be as easy as accept (the German supervisory authorities require that refusing consent is no harder than accepting it  a prominent \'Alle ablehnen\' (reject all) button is required). ClickMasters implements Usercentrics or OneTrust as the Consent Management Platform (CMP) for German websites, configured to the German-standard requirements.',
        },
      ],
